diff --git a/docs/spotify-redirect.html b/docs/spotify-redirect.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..98b84ff
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/spotify-redirect.html
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+<!DOCTYPE html>
+<html lang="en">
+<head>
+<meta charset="utf-8">
+<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
+<title>CrossPoint Spotify Bridge</title>
+</head>
+<body style="font-family:sans-serif;text-align:center;padding-top:3em">
+<h2 id="msg">Forwarding to your CrossPoint&hellip;</h2>
+<p id="detail"></p>
+<script>
+// Spotify requires HTTPS redirect URIs for new apps, but the CrossPoint device
+// only serves plain http on the LAN. Host this page on any HTTPS site (e.g.
+// GitHub Pages), register its URL as the app's Redirect URI, and it forwards
+// the OAuth response to the device. The device's LAN IP rides along in the
+// OAuth `state` parameter as "<csrf>~<ip>" (set by the firmware).
+(function () {
+  var params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
+  var state = params.get('state') || '';
+  var sep = state.indexOf('~');
+  var ip = sep >= 0 ? state.slice(sep + 1) : '';
+  // Only ever forward to a private LAN address.
+  var lan = /^(10\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|192\.168\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3}|172\.(1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\.\d{1,3}\.\d{1,3})$/;
+  if (!ip || !lan.test(ip)) {
+    document.getElementById('msg').textContent = 'Could not find your CrossPoint';
+    document.getElementById('detail').textContent =
+      'The link did not include a valid device address. Start the link again from the device.';
+    return;
+  }
+  location.replace('http://' + ip + '/spotify/callback' + location.search);
+})();
+</script>
+</body>
+</html>
diff --git a/docs/spotify.md b/docs/spotify.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..25051b0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/spotify.md
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+# Spotify "Now Playing" Mode
+
+Shows the album artwork (dithered for e-ink), track title, artists and a
+progress bar for whatever is currently playing on your Spotify account —
+on any device: phone, desktop, smart speaker. The reader only *observes*
+playback via the Spotify Web API; it does not play audio.
+
+Open it from the home menu: **Spotify**.
+
+## One-time setup
+
+You need a (free) Spotify application of your own — the mode talks to the
+Web API with *your* client ID and never shares an app with other users.
+
+1. Go to [developer.spotify.com/dashboard](https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard),
+   create an app (any name), and note its **Client ID**. Select **Web API**;
+   no client secret is needed (the device uses the PKCE flow).
+2. On the reader, open **Spotify** from the home menu and connect to WiFi.
+   The screen shows a QR code — scan it with your phone (same WiFi network).
+3. The setup page opens in your phone browser. Paste the **Client ID**.
+4. Add a **Redirect URI** to your Spotify app (dashboard → app → Settings)
+   and enter the same value on the setup page:
+   - **Device-direct** (try this first): `http://<device-ip>/spotify/callback`
+     — the setup page shows your device's exact address. Spotify has been
+     rejecting plain-http LAN addresses for newly created apps; if the
+     dashboard refuses to save it, use the bridge below.
+   - **HTTPS bridge**: host [`docs/spotify-redirect.html`](spotify-redirect.html)
+     on any HTTPS site you control — the easiest is enabling GitHub Pages on
+     your fork — and register that page's URL as the Redirect URI. The page
+     simply forwards Spotify's response to your device on the LAN (the
+     device's IP travels in the OAuth `state` parameter; the page refuses to
+     forward anywhere but a private LAN address).
+5. Tap **Save & Connect Spotify**, log in and approve. The reader screen
+   switches to Now Playing as soon as the link completes.
+
+The link is stored on the SD card (`/.crosspoint/spotify.json`, token
+obfuscated and device-bound) and survives reboots — after setup the mode
+just needs WiFi. Scopes requested: `user-read-currently-playing`,
+`user-read-playback-state`.
+
+## Using it
+
+- Artwork updates on track change (with a de-ghosting refresh); the progress
+  bar ticks with fast partial refreshes.
+- Polling: every 10s while playing, 30s when idle. **Confirm** forces an
+  immediate refresh; **Back** returns home.
+- Auto-sleep is disabled while the mode is open — it uses WiFi
+  continuously, so expect meaningfully higher battery drain than reading.
+- "Nothing playing" appears when no device is active (or during an ad).
+- To unlink the account: open the setup page (QR shown when the link is
+  broken, or re-run setup) and use **Unlink account**.
+
+## Implementation notes
+
+- `lib/Spotify/` — PKCE helpers (`SpotifyAuthUtils`, host-tested), the Web
+  API client (`SpotifyClient`, modeled on `KOReaderSyncClient`: small TLS
+  buffers + heap guard), the currently-playing JSON parser (filtered
+  ArduinoJson, host-tested) and the SD-card store (`SpotifyStore`).
+- `src/network/SpotifyAuthServer` — tiny HTTP server for the setup page and
+  the OAuth login/callback routes; only runs during setup/link.
+- `src/activities/network/SpotifyActivity` — the mode itself. Album art is
+  downloaded once per album (300px JPEG), converted to a dithered 1-bit BMP
+  at display size via `JpegToBmpConverter`, cached at
+  `/.crosspoint/spotify/art.bmp` and blitted on each render.
+- RAM: the outbound TLS session (~48KB during handshake) never overlaps the
+  JPEG decode; both are guarded by free-heap checks and the response JSON is
+  filtered to under ~1KB.
